WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.393
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4547.

2
00:00:01.393 --> 00:00:02.786
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.786 --> 00:00:05.571
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Alright well it is November seventeenth . . .

4
00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:06.036
<v JUDY MONTGOMERY-GAUTHIA>Seventeenth.

5
00:00:06.036 --> 00:00:07.429
<v DIGILORMO>Two thousand fifteen.

6
00:00:07.429 --> 00:00:12.071
We're here at 313 Prater Road with Ms. Judy Montgomery-Gauthia.

7
00:00:12.071 --> 00:00:13.000
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

8
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.127
<v DIGILORMO>And we are, let’s see, sitting here hoping that the weather doesn't get too bad.

9
00:00:19.127 --> 00:00:19.535
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

10
00:00:19.535 --> 00:00:21.986
<v DIGILORMO>It's a pretty nasty day out.

11
00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:24.028
The wind is kicking so . . .

12
00:00:24.028 --> 00:00:24.845
<v GAUTHIA>Yes, Lord.

13
00:00:24.845 --> 00:00:30.972
<v DIGILORMO>Hopefully we won't get . . . have to bunker down here in the middle of the interview.

14
00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:31.380
<v GAUTHIA>Right.

15
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.831
No electricity, no lights, no nothing.

16
00:00:33.831 --> 00:00:34.239
<v DIGILORMO>Right!

17
00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:34.648
Right.

18
00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:37.915
That would really put a damper on things.

19
00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:41.183
Well so I've got a series of questions.

20
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:42.000
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

21
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.756
<v DIGILORMO>And they’re all sort of broken down into different categories.

22
00:00:46.756 --> 00:00:53.415
So we'll start with your childhood and kind of move up through young adulthood,

23
00:00:53.415 --> 00:01:01.024
and into your being an adult and things like that and how it relates to Mossville.

24
00:01:01.024 --> 00:01:01.500
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

25
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:09.585
<v DIGILORMO>So first question I wanted to ask you about is the home that you were raised in.

26
00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:10.061
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

27
00:01:10.061 --> 00:01:12.915
<v DIGILORMO>So could you describe that home?

28
00:01:12.915 --> 00:01:13.390
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

29
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.817
It's gone now.

30
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:15.293
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

31
00:01:15.293 --> 00:01:16.244
<v GAUTHIA>Through Sasol.

32
00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:18.146
They bought it out.

33
00:01:18.146 --> 00:01:18.622
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

34
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:21.000
Where was that located?

35
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.522
<v GAUTHIA>Right here one Prater Road.

36
00:01:22.522 --> 00:01:24.652
Right down the street to the right.

37
00:01:24.652 --> 00:01:25.261
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, okay.

38
00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:27.391
<v GAUTHIA>It was . . . Rodney [Caesar?] had owned it.

39
00:01:27.391 --> 00:01:32.565
Or the family because he had done already passed away before they got a chance to sell.

40
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:35.000
<v DIGILORMO>So this wasn't the house that y’all were . . .?

41
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.826
<v GAUTHIA>That we was raised in [disagrees].

42
00:01:36.826 --> 00:01:37.130
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

43
00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.348
I thought it was.

44
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:41.391
<v GAUTHIA>No. So it was, I guess, fairly a big house.

45
00:01:41.391 --> 00:01:42.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

46
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.364
<v GAUTHIA>And they . . . We was five in the family.

47
00:01:46.364 --> 00:01:48.000
Raise up [Keat?].

48
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.182
Go up on it.

49
00:01:50.182 --> 00:01:50.727
And . . .

50
00:01:50.727 --> 00:01:51.818
<v DIGILORMO>Five children?

51
00:01:51.818 --> 00:01:52.364
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

52
00:01:52.364 --> 00:01:53.455
Five children.

53
00:01:53.455 --> 00:01:54.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

54
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.091
<v GAUTHIA>And my parents . . . of course my parents always was taking someone in so . . .

55
00:02:01.091 --> 00:02:01.636
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

56
00:02:01.636 --> 00:02:04.364
<v GAUTHIA>But other than just us,

57
00:02:04.364 --> 00:02:14.727
it was just . . . I guess we got along because my parents didn't believe in us fighting amongst ourselves or . . .

58
00:02:14.727 --> 00:02:15.273
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

59
00:02:15.273 --> 00:02:17.455
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, sorry about that.

60
00:02:17.455 --> 00:02:18.545
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, no.

61
00:02:18.545 --> 00:02:22.909
<v GAUTHIA>We . . . I was kind of the easy-going person.

62
00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:24.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

63
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.369
<v GAUTHIA>I didn't like to catch whippings, so I didn't buck the system.

64
00:02:28.369 --> 00:02:28.733
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

65
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:29.097
<v GAUTHIA>So . . .

66
00:02:29.097 --> 00:02:33.831
<v DIGILORMO>So did you have chores and things like that you had to do?

67
00:02:33.831 --> 00:02:34.559
<v GAUTHIA>Oh yeah.

68
00:02:34.559 --> 00:02:34.923
Yeah.

69
00:02:34.923 --> 00:02:37.108
Mama gave us things to do.

70
00:02:37.108 --> 00:02:40.749
I was one of these, I'm never in a hurry.

71
00:02:40.749 --> 00:02:42.205
I'm always moving slow.

72
00:02:42.205 --> 00:02:42.569
Nonchalant.

73
00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:43.662
Nothing bothers me.

74
00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:46.210
I always say I can do it.

75
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.303
I got time.

76
00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:49.487
I'll do it later type thing.

77
00:02:49.487 --> 00:02:54.949
My sister Sally, she was on the fast side so she moved faster than me.

78
00:02:54.949 --> 00:03:02.595
She believed in getting her chores done. [laughs] Then Mama looked around seen still got some stuff need to be done.

79
00:03:02.595 --> 00:03:07.328
She don't look at it like it's just for Judy to do now.

80
00:03:07.328 --> 00:03:09.877
She be mad at both of us.

81
00:03:09.877 --> 00:03:14.974
And Sally’d be trying to explain to her, "No, Mama I did my work.

82
00:03:14.974 --> 00:03:16.067
That's for Judy."

83
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.708
And boy she'd get a slap. [laughs] Stuff like that.

84
00:03:19.708 --> 00:03:24.077
So I didn't catch no slapping because I'd just say, "Okay, Mama.

85
00:03:24.077 --> 00:03:25.169
I'm going coming.

86
00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:26.262
I'm doing it.

87
00:03:26.262 --> 00:03:28.082
I'm going to do it."

88
00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:31.359
<v DIGILORMO>What kind of chores did y'all have to do?

89
00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:34.272
<v GAUTHIA>Like washing the dishes, ironing, stuff like that.

90
00:03:34.272 --> 00:03:35.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

91
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.349
<v GAUTHIA>Maybe she was trying to teach us a little cooking, too.

92
00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:40.535
I don't know.

93
00:03:40.535 --> 00:03:40.930
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

94
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:41.326
Okay.

95
00:03:41.326 --> 00:03:42.116
<v GAUTHIA>Type thing.

96
00:03:42.116 --> 00:03:45.279
<v DIGILORMO>Did you learn to cook from your mom?

97
00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:46.860
<v GAUTHIA>Just a very little.

98
00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.419
Not . . . I wasn't . . . My mama was a good cook.

99
00:03:50.419 --> 00:03:52.395
Her and my dad both.

100
00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:55.163
But I didn't learn how to cook.

101
00:03:55.163 --> 00:03:56.744
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, I got you.

102
00:03:56.744 --> 00:04:00.698
<v GAUTHIA>I don't like cooking, so cooking is not my dish.

103
00:04:00.698 --> 00:04:04.256
I didn't mind being a helper in the kitchen.

104
00:04:04.256 --> 00:04:06.233
But I don't like cooking.

105
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:08.209
But sometime I have to . . .

106
00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:09.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

107
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.539
<v GAUTHIA>But it's, like my mother she loved to cook.

108
00:04:12.539 --> 00:04:15.292
It didn't take her long to cook.

109
00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:16.865
<c.p>Just like y'all here.

110
00:04:16.865 --> 00:04:21.191
She'll slip off and she'll pull something out the deep freezer.

111
00:04:21.191 --> 00:04:21.978
Start cooking.

112
00:04:21.978 --> 00:04:24.730
That's the type of lady she was.

113
00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:25.124
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

114
00:04:25.124 --> 00:04:27.483
<v GAUTHIA>And she liked to feed people.

115
00:04:27.483 --> 00:04:29.449
She liked to feed everybody.

116
00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:29.843
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

117
00:04:29.843 --> 00:04:31.809
<v GAUTHIA>Talk to anybody in Mossville.

118
00:04:31.809 --> 00:04:36.135
They probably done all ate over here at my parents' place.

119
00:04:36.135 --> 00:04:36.528
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

120
00:04:36.528 --> 00:04:38.888
<v GAUTHIA>So that's how we was raised.

121
00:04:38.888 --> 00:04:42.034
<v DIGILORMO>So your parents . . . Your father worked for Conoco?

122
00:04:42.034 --> 00:04:42.427
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

123
00:04:42.427 --> 00:04:44.000
At the plant.

124
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.956
<v DIGILORMO>And your mother took care of the home or does she work outside of the home?

125
00:04:48.956 --> 00:04:53.292
<v GAUTHIA>Well she took care, and she kind of worked a little outside the home.

126
00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.841
I don't remember exactly when.

127
00:04:54.841 --> 00:04:57.938
But then my parents had went into the nightclub business.

128
00:04:57.938 --> 00:05:01.035
And Mama used to do that because Daddy was working.

129
00:05:01.035 --> 00:05:01.345
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

130
00:05:01.345 --> 00:05:02.894
<v GAUTHIA>So that's how that was.

131
00:05:02.894 --> 00:05:04.752
But I don't remember what year.

132
00:05:04.752 --> 00:05:05.062
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

133
00:05:05.062 --> 00:05:06.920
<v GAUTHIA>Maybe my brother or somebody else.

134
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.779
Sally maybe, but I don't remember.

135
00:05:08.779 --> 00:05:14.664
<v DIGILORMO>Did you have a lot of children on the road or in the neighborhood that you would play with?

136
00:05:14.664 --> 00:05:19.000
Were there a lot of families at the time you were growing up?

137
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.965
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah, but Mama kind of liked to leave us stay in the house or in the yard.

138
00:05:23.965 --> 00:05:25.717
She didn't like us to roam.

139
00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:26.009
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

140
00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:27.761
<v GAUTHIA>My brothers, they would sneak off.

141
00:05:27.761 --> 00:05:29.805
They was I guess kind of rascals.

142
00:05:29.805 --> 00:05:35.354
They would sneak off and go playing, but Sally and we I more had to stay in the house.

143
00:05:35.354 --> 00:05:37.398
She didn't care for us going out.

144
00:05:37.398 --> 00:05:38.858
Maybe because we was female.

145
00:05:38.858 --> 00:05:42.071
She really didn't want none of us to go to [. . .?] but

146
00:05:42.071 --> 00:05:43.531
<c.p>the boys would catch whippings.

147
00:05:43.531 --> 00:05:46.159
They used to go swimming in a dynamite hole.

148
00:05:46.159 --> 00:05:47.912
I don't know where that was.

149
00:05:47.912 --> 00:05:48.788
I never went.

150
00:05:48.788 --> 00:05:50.248
<v DIGILORMO>What is a dynamite hole?

151
00:05:50.248 --> 00:05:50.832
Just a . . .

152
00:05:50.832 --> 00:05:52.000
<v GAUTHIA>A big hole.

153
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.364
<v DIGILORMO>A crater left from some explosion or something?

154
00:05:54.364 --> 00:06:00.273
<v GAUTHIA>I don't know why they . . . How they got that name I don't know, but I can imagine something like that.

155
00:06:00.273 --> 00:06:05.000
Some kind of big hole right here in Mossville and they go swimming out there.

156
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.761
<v DIGILORMO>So what'd you do?

157
00:06:06.761 --> 00:06:13.367
What did you and your sister do for fun if y'all weren't able to roam?

158
00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:14.688
<v GAUTHIA>I don't know.

159
00:06:14.688 --> 00:06:17.771
Mama always kept us busy doing something.

160
00:06:17.771 --> 00:06:18.211
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

161
00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:23.936
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah, so . . . And then I always worked so slow, so [phone makes noise].

162
00:06:23.936 --> 00:06:27.899
<v DIGILORMO>You want us to pause so you can answer?

163
00:06:27.899 --> 00:06:29.220
<v GAUTHIA>[whispers] [don’t matter?]

164
00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:29.661
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

165
00:06:29.661 --> 00:06:35.385
So let's see . . . I guess this sort of follows on the last question.

166
00:06:35.385 --> 00:06:41.991
I had wanted to ask you what you did for fun as a young child.

167
00:06:41.991 --> 00:06:42.431
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

168
00:06:42.431 --> 00:06:42.872
Okay.

169
00:06:42.872 --> 00:06:48.156
<v DIGILORMO>You say you had mostly chores to do to keep you busy.

170
00:06:48.156 --> 00:06:48.596
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

171
00:06:48.596 --> 00:06:49.037
Yeah.

172
00:06:49.037 --> 00:06:53.000
<v DIGILORMO>Was there anything you looked forward to doing?

173
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.676
<v GAUTHIA>No, I guess I never did have a high goals or things like that.

174
00:06:58.676 --> 00:07:01.919
I just took one day at a time.

175
00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:02.324
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

176
00:07:02.324 --> 00:07:03.946
<v GAUTHIA>I just never did . . .

177
00:07:03.946 --> 00:07:06.378
<v DIGILORMO>No hobbies or anything like that?

178
00:07:06.378 --> 00:07:08.811
<v GAUTHIA>No. I don't remember any hobbies.

179
00:07:08.811 --> 00:07:09.216
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

180
00:07:09.216 --> 00:07:12.865
<v GAUTHIA>We had to help my parents in the club.

181
00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:16.108
They would let us do little things like . . .

182
00:07:16.108 --> 00:07:19.757
<v DIGILORMO>So when did you start working or helping out?

183
00:07:19.757 --> 00:07:22.189
<v GAUTHIA>I guess just real young like . . .

184
00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:23.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

185
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:30.174
<v GAUTHIA>Sometime they would instead of go walk all the way to the club they'd bring some beer and put it in the refrigerator.

186
00:07:30.174 --> 00:07:33.606
Some little half of pints they'd put it in the bedroom.

187
00:07:33.606 --> 00:07:38.908
And they would just ask us to go get it out the box or out the bedroom.

188
00:07:38.908 --> 00:07:43.275
So that's what I was say we was working at a real young age.

189
00:07:43.275 --> 00:07:44.211
I don't remember.

190
00:07:44.211 --> 00:07:45.459
Probably illegal, too. [laughs]

191
00:07:45.459 --> 00:07:46.394
<v DIGILORMO>So Club Paradise.

192
00:07:46.394 --> 00:07:48.890
That was the name of your parent's club?

193
00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:49.202
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

194
00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:52.009
<v DIGILORMO>How far was that walking distance from your house?

195
00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:54.193
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, it was in the same yard.

196
00:07:54.193 --> 00:07:55.128
<v DIGILORMO>The same yard?

197
00:07:55.128 --> 00:07:55.440
Okay.

198
00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:55.752
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

199
00:07:55.752 --> 00:07:56.064
<v DIGILORMO>Alright.

200
00:07:56.064 --> 00:07:56.376
Wow.

201
00:07:56.376 --> 00:07:57.000
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

202
00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:57.703
<v DIGILORMO>Did it . . .

203
00:07:57.703 --> 00:08:01.919
<v GAUTHIA>And then after a while then they started having the dance hall.

204
00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:07.541
Then we were working there because by then we was bigger and we would work there.

205
00:08:07.541 --> 00:08:10.351
And everybody either have to wait tables or . . .

206
00:08:10.351 --> 00:08:10.703
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

207
00:08:10.703 --> 00:08:11.757
<v GAUTHIA>Work the bars.

208
00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.162
Work at the door.

209
00:08:13.162 --> 00:08:18.081
<v DIGILORMO>So it was kind of like a bar before it became a dance hall?

210
00:08:18.081 --> 00:08:18.432
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

211
00:08:18.432 --> 00:08:18.784
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

212
00:08:18.784 --> 00:08:19.135
Alright.

213
00:08:19.135 --> 00:08:23.000
Would you had called it a juke joint at first?

214
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:23.393
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

215
00:08:23.393 --> 00:08:24.571
I guess so.

216
00:08:24.571 --> 00:08:25.750
Something like that.

217
00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:26.929
<v DIGILORMO>Something like that.

218
00:08:26.929 --> 00:08:28.893
So they still had music?

219
00:08:28.893 --> 00:08:29.679
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, yeah.

220
00:08:29.679 --> 00:08:35.571
<v DIGILORMO>But it wasn't like a big . . . Where people went and danced and things like that?

221
00:08:35.571 --> 00:08:35.964
<v GAUTHIA>Right.

222
00:08:35.964 --> 00:08:37.929
It was a good-sized club.

223
00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.286
The dance hall was real big.

224
00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:45.393
It could hold thousands of people where the club couldn't hold that many.

225
00:08:45.393 --> 00:08:45.786
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

226
00:08:45.786 --> 00:08:46.964
<v GAUTHIA>A few hundreds.

227
00:08:46.964 --> 00:08:48.536
That's how it was.

228
00:08:48.536 --> 00:08:51.286
We just helped out with our parents.

229
00:08:51.286 --> 00:08:54.036
That's how we was raised to do.

230
00:08:54.036 --> 00:08:54.429
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

231
00:08:54.429 --> 00:08:56.000
<v GAUTHIA>Just to help.

232
00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.000
<v DIGILORMO>I've heard that's there's been a lot of famous people come through that club.

233
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:00.286
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

234
00:09:00.286 --> 00:09:04.000
<v DIGILORMO>Do you have any memories or were you too young to recognize . . .?

235
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:13.143
<v GAUTHIA>I can remember like Bobby Blue Bland, he sang beautifully, and I can remember him being a heavy set guy.

236
00:09:13.143 --> 00:09:14.971
Nice looking, beautiful face.

237
00:09:14.971 --> 00:09:19.086
That's all my parents and them would talk about.

238
00:09:19.086 --> 00:09:20.457
Especially my dad.

239
00:09:20.457 --> 00:09:22.743
He would be so excited.

240
00:09:22.743 --> 00:09:25.486
And Otis Redding, and Johnnie Taylor.

241
00:09:25.486 --> 00:09:26.400
Tina Turner.

242
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:33.714
Just everybody I guess they could think of to try to get them to the club.

243
00:09:33.714 --> 00:09:36.000
To the dance hall.

244
00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.880
<v DIGILORMO>Did you have any experiences with any of these musicians that you can remember?

245
00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.980
<v GAUTHIA>Well I was real young.

246
00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:44.400
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

247
00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:44.820
Right.

248
00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:51.540
<v GAUTHIA>Maybe get them a glass of water, or maybe if they wanted a beer or whatever.

249
00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:52.800
Stuff like that.

250
00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:53.220
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

251
00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.900
<v GAUTHIA>Stuff . . . Just simple things.

252
00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:55.320
<v DIGILORMO>Sure.

253
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.000
That's pretty amazing.

254
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:57.330
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

255
00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:04.268
After I was big enough to go to a club, I always said I didn't need to go to the club.

256
00:10:04.268 --> 00:10:06.250
I was raised in a club.

257
00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:07.902
I had enough of it.

258
00:10:07.902 --> 00:10:08.232
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

259
00:10:08.232 --> 00:10:09.554
<v GAUTHIA>Like people be thrilled.

260
00:10:09.554 --> 00:10:11.866
Say, "Oh, I'm going to the club.

261
00:10:11.866 --> 00:10:14.179
I'm old enough to go in now."

262
00:10:14.179 --> 00:10:19.464
That was never my highlight because I was raised in it and didn't care for it.

263
00:10:19.464 --> 00:10:20.455
<v DIGILORMO>Used to it.

264
00:10:20.455 --> 00:10:21.446
<v GAUTHIA>Used to it.

265
00:10:21.446 --> 00:10:22.438
It was common.

266
00:10:22.438 --> 00:10:24.089
There was nothing to it.

267
00:10:24.089 --> 00:10:24.420
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

268
00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.062
Are there any particular memories that you have?

269
00:10:27.062 --> 00:10:34.000
Like not just of famous people, but just anything special or memorable that happened to you at that time working?

270
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.451
<v GAUTHIA>I met my husband.

271
00:10:35.451 --> 00:10:36.176
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, okay.

272
00:10:36.176 --> 00:10:37.264
That's pretty memorable.

273
00:10:37.264 --> 00:10:37.626
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

274
00:10:37.626 --> 00:10:41.253
My parents and them would have bingo at the hall.

275
00:10:41.253 --> 00:10:41.615
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

276
00:10:41.615 --> 00:10:43.791
<v GAUTHIA>And they wasn't giving a bingo.

277
00:10:43.791 --> 00:10:51.769
They had let some organization come in and they would give it, but we would work the tables and stuff like that.

278
00:10:51.769 --> 00:10:54.308
So my husband was named Larry Gauthia.

279
00:10:54.308 --> 00:10:56.121
That was my boyfriend whatever.

280
00:10:56.121 --> 00:10:57.571
I met him there.

281
00:10:57.571 --> 00:11:05.187
Anyway and he . . . I went to wait on his table and he gave me a tip, and I was so excited.

282
00:11:05.187 --> 00:11:06.637
So I say, "Wow."

283
00:11:06.637 --> 00:11:12.440
I say, "Whenever he needs something else I'm going to catch that table one more time."

284
00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.978
I was tickled to get a tip.

285
00:11:14.978 --> 00:11:20.418
So my brother was about to go catch that table and I called him back.

286
00:11:20.418 --> 00:11:23.681
I said, "No, no, no. I'm getting that table."

287
00:11:23.681 --> 00:11:26.220
I say, "He gave me a tip.

288
00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.033
I'm going to go back."

289
00:11:28.033 --> 00:11:33.835
So when I went back he asked me for my phone number and stuff like that.

290
00:11:33.835 --> 00:11:36.374
So we had been talking ever since.

291
00:11:36.374 --> 00:11:36.736
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

292
00:11:36.736 --> 00:11:37.099
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

293
00:11:37.099 --> 00:11:40.000
<v DIGILORMO>How old were you at that time?

294
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:40.538
<v GAUTHIA>Jesus.

295
00:11:40.538 --> 00:11:47.000
Probably . . . I think I dated maybe . . . I got married on my birthday.

296
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:48.077
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, nice.

297
00:11:48.077 --> 00:11:51.846
<v GAUTHIA>June the fourteenth, nineteen . . . What was it?

298
00:11:51.846 --> 00:11:54.000
I done forgot now.

299
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.077
Let's see.

300
00:11:55.077 --> 00:11:55.615
‘72,

301
00:11:55.615 --> 00:12:01.000
<c.p>so I guess we dated maybe two or three years.

302
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.692
I forgot how many years.

303
00:12:03.692 --> 00:12:09.615
So just say it was three years before I got married.

304
00:12:09.615 --> 00:12:18.231
So I got married . . . I got married on my birthday so I was twenty-one or twenty-two.

305
00:12:18.231 --> 00:12:19.846
Something like that.

306
00:12:19.846 --> 00:12:21.462
I done forgot.

307
00:12:21.462 --> 00:12:22.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

308
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.231
<v GAUTHIA>I used to could remember that.

309
00:12:25.231 --> 00:12:27.385
But give or take.

310
00:12:27.385 --> 00:12:30.615
I was either twenty-one or twenty-two.

311
00:12:30.615 --> 00:12:36.000
So I would say about three years before then.

312
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:41.140
<v DIGILORMO>So you were working at the club while you were going to school?

313
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:41.930
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, yeah.

314
00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:42.326
Yeah.

315
00:12:42.326 --> 00:12:42.721
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

316
00:12:42.721 --> 00:12:43.116
Wow.

317
00:12:43.116 --> 00:12:45.488
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah, so that's how that was.

318
00:12:45.488 --> 00:12:48.256
<v DIGILORMO>That must have been a little exhausting.

319
00:12:48.256 --> 00:12:51.023
<v GAUTHIA>Well, no. They wouldn't keep us out.

320
00:12:51.023 --> 00:12:51.814
<v DIGILORMO>Too late?

321
00:12:51.814 --> 00:12:52.209
<v GAUTHIA>No.

322
00:12:52.209 --> 00:12:53.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

323
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:58.596
<v GAUTHIA>And I had my graduation party there when I graduated in '69.

324
00:12:58.596 --> 00:13:06.056
That was the last year Mossville School had, you know, shut down for the high school.

325
00:13:06.056 --> 00:13:06.522
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

326
00:13:06.522 --> 00:13:08.854
<v GAUTHIA>So that was in 1969.

327
00:13:08.854 --> 00:13:09.320
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

328
00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:20.511
Well, so going back to your younger years when you lived with your family and growing up as a young child and young teenager,

329
00:13:20.511 --> 00:13:24.708
where did your family get food and groceries from?

330
00:13:24.708 --> 00:13:31.702
<v GAUTHIA>At one time I imagine, times was hard and they would go and buy groceries.

331
00:13:31.702 --> 00:13:37.764
I remember they had a little grocery store right here on Prater Road.

332
00:13:37.764 --> 00:13:38.697
<v DIGILORMO>Oh really?

333
00:13:38.697 --> 00:13:44.758
<v GAUTHIA>[Godou's?] and it had some other name, too. [Trahan?] I think or something.

334
00:13:44.758 --> 00:13:47.556
But they would . . . We’d go there.

335
00:13:47.556 --> 00:13:51.287
Walk to the store and get little things.

336
00:13:51.287 --> 00:13:55.483
Then sometimes they'd get more things in Westlake [Louisiana].

337
00:13:55.483 --> 00:13:55.949
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

338
00:13:55.949 --> 00:14:07.140
<v GAUTHIA>And they would let them . . . I can remember my parents would have like . . . sign a book and they would buy their grocery like that.

339
00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:07.607
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

340
00:14:07.607 --> 00:14:16.000
<v GAUTHIA>Then I guess certain time of the two weeks or whatever they would go pay they bill.

341
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.231
<v DIGILORMO>So kind of like on a credit?

342
00:14:19.231 --> 00:14:19.692
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

343
00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:20.615
Credit bill.

344
00:14:20.615 --> 00:14:21.077
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

345
00:14:21.077 --> 00:14:21.538
Okay.

346
00:14:21.538 --> 00:14:22.000
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

347
00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.615
<v DIGILORMO>Was the Westlake grocery store pretty far or was it?

348
00:14:26.615 --> 00:14:27.077
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

349
00:14:27.077 --> 00:14:28.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

350
00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.462
<v GAUTHIA>I'm pretty sure we had to go in a car.

351
00:14:31.462 --> 00:14:31.808
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

352
00:14:31.808 --> 00:14:36.308
Do you know if it was owned by white people or black people?

353
00:14:36.308 --> 00:14:36.654
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

354
00:14:36.654 --> 00:14:37.346
Oh, yes.

355
00:14:37.346 --> 00:14:37.692
White.

356
00:14:37.692 --> 00:14:38.731
<v DIGILORMO>Okay, it was?

357
00:14:38.731 --> 00:14:39.077
Alright.

358
00:14:39.077 --> 00:14:41.154
I was just wondering real quick.

359
00:14:41.154 --> 00:14:46.000
So did they raise . . . Did they have gardens or raise animals at all?

360
00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.250
<v GAUTHIA>I can't remember.

361
00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:47.667
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

362
00:14:47.667 --> 00:14:48.917
So probably not.

363
00:14:48.917 --> 00:14:51.833
If they did it wasn't too extensive.

364
00:14:51.833 --> 00:14:52.250
<v GAUTHIA>Right.

365
00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:53.917
<v DIGILORMO>If you don't remember.

366
00:14:53.917 --> 00:14:55.167
<v GAUTHIA>I don't remember.

367
00:14:55.167 --> 00:14:56.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

368
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.123
<v GAUTHIA>But I remember hanging up clothes on the outside on the line. [laughs] You had to go get it in.

369
00:15:03.123 --> 00:15:08.466
If it threatened rain you had to hurry up and go bring the clothes in.

370
00:15:08.466 --> 00:15:08.822
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

371
00:15:08.822 --> 00:15:11.671
So how did y'all wash clothes back then?

372
00:15:11.671 --> 00:15:13.808
<v GAUTHIA>Rubbing board on first by hand.

373
00:15:13.808 --> 00:15:15.589
Rubbing board in the tub.

374
00:15:15.589 --> 00:15:20.932
Then eventually my daddy said he wanted to get my mother an automatic washing machine.

375
00:15:20.932 --> 00:15:22.000
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, wow.

376
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.088
<v GAUTHIA>And Mama said, "Well I don't want no fancy thing."

377
00:15:25.088 --> 00:15:27.559
She said, "Just give me that old-fashioned thing."

378
00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:30.029
And she wanted the kind that you turn.

379
00:15:30.029 --> 00:15:34.353
It had two little wheels and you turn it and you pass your clothes.

380
00:15:34.353 --> 00:15:34.971
<v DIGILORMO>Through it.

381
00:15:34.971 --> 00:15:35.279
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

382
00:15:35.279 --> 00:15:37.132
Through it and stuff like that.

383
00:15:37.132 --> 00:15:42.074
Daddy said, "No I want to buy you something good that you don't have to work."

384
00:15:42.074 --> 00:15:46.397
You just put your clothes in and your detergent and let it wash automatic.

385
00:15:46.397 --> 00:15:49.485
And Mom’s saying, “I don't know about all of that."

386
00:15:49.485 --> 00:15:51.647
She said, "That might break on me."

387
00:15:51.647 --> 00:15:52.882
<v DIGILORMO>She's a little skeptical.

388
00:15:52.882 --> 00:15:53.191
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

389
00:15:53.191 --> 00:15:55.662
But eventually they did get the new kind.

390
00:15:55.662 --> 00:15:55.971
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

391
00:15:55.971 --> 00:15:58.132
<v GAUTHIA>And a dryer and stuff like that.

392
00:15:58.132 --> 00:16:01.529
They didn't have to go outside and hang out clothes anymore.

393
00:16:01.529 --> 00:16:01.838
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

394
00:16:01.838 --> 00:16:04.000
I bet that was a relief.

395
00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:04.342
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

396
00:16:04.342 --> 00:16:07.423
<v DIGILORMO>So did your family ever like, hunt or fish?

397
00:16:07.423 --> 00:16:10.847
<v GAUTHIA>No, I think my daddy maybe did a little hunting.

398
00:16:10.847 --> 00:16:11.189
Maybe.

399
00:16:11.189 --> 00:16:15.982
I know when they was younger, but I don't think it was in Mossville.

400
00:16:15.982 --> 00:16:16.324
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

401
00:16:16.324 --> 00:16:21.459
<v GAUTHIA>Because they . . . I can remember somebody said that they didn't have no gun or nothing.

402
00:16:21.459 --> 00:16:25.568
They would run and try to catch the rabbit with a stick.

403
00:16:25.568 --> 00:16:26.252
Hit the . . .

404
00:16:26.252 --> 00:16:26.595
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

405
00:16:26.595 --> 00:16:27.622
<v GAUTHIA>With a stick.

406
00:16:27.622 --> 00:16:32.757
And that's how they would kill it and bring it and cook it at home.

407
00:16:32.757 --> 00:16:33.099
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

408
00:16:33.099 --> 00:16:36.865
<v GAUTHIA>So you had to be kind of fast on your feet.

409
00:16:36.865 --> 00:16:40.631
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah, and have some talent to sneak up on a rabbit.

410
00:16:40.631 --> 00:16:41.315
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah, yeah.

411
00:16:41.315 --> 00:16:42.000
So . . .

412
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.554
<v DIGILORMO>So that's mostly what they would hunt is rabbit?

413
00:16:44.554 --> 00:16:46.541
Nothing like squirrel, or deer, or duck?

414
00:16:46.541 --> 00:16:49.662
<v GAUTHIA>Oh I don't know, but I can remember them saying that.

415
00:16:49.662 --> 00:16:49.946
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

416
00:16:49.946 --> 00:16:50.797
<v GAUTHIA>That I remember.

417
00:16:50.797 --> 00:16:51.081
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

418
00:16:51.081 --> 00:16:52.500
Oh, and what about fishing?

419
00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:54.203
Was fishing ever a big deal?

420
00:16:54.203 --> 00:16:55.338
Y'all ever eat fish?

421
00:16:55.338 --> 00:16:57.608
<v GAUTHIA>I don't think . . . Yeah my parents loved it,

422
00:16:57.608 --> 00:17:02.432
but my parents don't know how to swim so they didn’t . . . They were scared of water, so . . .

423
00:17:02.432 --> 00:17:03.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

424
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.875
<v GAUTHIA>They was not a fisherman.

425
00:17:04.875 --> 00:17:05.250
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

426
00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.125
<v GAUTHIA>They didn't believe in that.

427
00:17:07.125 --> 00:17:12.375
<v DIGILORMO>What about . . . I've talked to a few families who have gone crabbing down in . . .

428
00:17:12.375 --> 00:17:13.125
<v GAUTHIA>In Cameron?

429
00:17:13.125 --> 00:17:15.000
<v DIGILORMO>In Cameron Parish [Louisiana].

430
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.600
<v GAUTHIA>That's what I said.

431
00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:20.200
They maybe went buy some but they didn't go . . .

432
00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:21.800
<v DIGILORMO>They didn't go crabbing.

433
00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:22.200
Yeah.

434
00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:22.600
<v GAUTHIA>No.

435
00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:23.800
<v DIGILORMO>I got you.

436
00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:27.400
So y'all would eat seafood occasionally from down there?

437
00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:27.800
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

438
00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:32.600
And you remember years ago the Catholic couldn't eat meat on Friday.

439
00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:33.000
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

440
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.200
<v GAUTHIA>Or Good Friday.

441
00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:35.000
Easter time.

442
00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.600
So I can remember us . . . Would have seafood then.

443
00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.600
If parents didn't have seafood they would give us egg.

444
00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:43.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

445
00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:49.400
Did the people of Mossville ever cater to, like say, little maybe grocery stores, or delis,

446
00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:55.000
or restaurants . . . Would they ever cater to Catholics who couldn't eat meat on Friday?

447
00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:56.200
Do you remember?

448
00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.400
<v GAUTHIA>Well the school.

449
00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:59.000
<v DIGILORMO>The school would?

450
00:17:59.000 --> 00:17:59.476
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

451
00:17:59.476 --> 00:18:09.952
When we’d go to school they wouldn't . . . I guess for the ones that was Catholic you can have I guess like seafood.

452
00:18:09.952 --> 00:18:10.429
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

453
00:18:10.429 --> 00:18:16.143
How . . . What percentage do you think Mossville is in terms of Catholics?

454
00:18:16.143 --> 00:18:19.000
Seems like there's a lot.

455
00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:19.474
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

456
00:18:19.474 --> 00:18:25.167
I would assume the Catholics was more the bigger . . . the higher percentage.

457
00:18:25.167 --> 00:18:25.641
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

458
00:18:25.641 --> 00:18:28.487
<v GAUTHIA>I guess maybe Baptist after that.

459
00:18:28.487 --> 00:18:32.282
<v DIGILORMO>But there's not a Catholic church here, right?

460
00:18:32.282 --> 00:18:35.128
Y'all had to go to Westlake?

461
00:18:35.128 --> 00:18:37.500
<v GAUTHIA>Westlake to go to school.

462
00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:37.974
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

463
00:18:37.974 --> 00:18:39.872
<v GAUTHIA>To go to church.

464
00:18:39.872 --> 00:18:40.346
Yeah.

465
00:18:40.346 --> 00:18:41.295
<v DIGILORMO>That's interesting.

466
00:18:41.295 --> 00:18:45.564
Okay so we're moving on to Mossville the school.

467
00:18:45.564 --> 00:18:56.000
Do you have some of your . . . some first memories of going to the school when you were very young starting out?

468
00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.297
<v GAUTHIA>To tell the truth I don't remember a whole lot.

469
00:18:59.297 --> 00:19:04.901
Can't remember my teachers and stuff, but I can remember I had to repeat the third grade.

470
00:19:04.901 --> 00:19:05.890
That I remember.

471
00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:06.549
<v DIGILORMO>Oh yeah.

472
00:19:06.549 --> 00:19:16.440
<v GAUTHIA>I think I had a ringworm and Mama done kept me out of school because it catches and I guess they didn't want me to pass it to anybody else,

473
00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:20.066
so they put me out or whatever the case may be.

474
00:19:20.066 --> 00:19:22.703
They keep you out of school for that.

475
00:19:22.703 --> 00:19:23.033
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

476
00:19:23.033 --> 00:19:25.341
<v GAUTHIA>I had to repeat the third grade.

477
00:19:25.341 --> 00:19:26.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

478
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.725
<v GAUTHIA>But other than that I guess everything else went okay.

479
00:19:29.725 --> 00:19:30.843
<v DIGILORMO>You enjoyed school?

480
00:19:30.843 --> 00:19:31.961
<v GAUTHIA>I think so.

481
00:19:31.961 --> 00:19:32.706
Pretty much.

482
00:19:32.706 --> 00:19:40.157
